      <p>Few searches are more urgent than "water damage restoration near me". The searcher has water on the floor or through the ceiling and needs someone now. That urgency makes the work valuable and highly competitive, and it means the speed of your response matters as much as the quality of your ads.</p>

      <h2>Built around the first phone call</h2>
      <p>In restoration, the job usually goes to the first company that answers and can arrive soonest. Every part of the campaign should support that moment.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Calls answered around the clock, or ads scheduled only for the hours they are</li>
        <li>Call assets and call-focused ads, because most people in an emergency ring rather than fill in a form</li>
        <li>Landing pages with the phone number at the top and a clear promise about response time, where you can keep it</li>
        <li>Call tracking that shows missed and short calls, so lost opportunities are visible</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Separate the emergencies from the rest</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Water damage and flooding:</strong> the emergency core, usually the largest budget</li>
        <li><strong>Mould remediation:</strong> less urgent, more research, different value</li>
        <li><strong>Fire and smoke damage:</strong> lower volume, high value, longer jobs</li>
        <li><strong>Commercial restoration</strong>, if you offer it, with its own buyers and contracts</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Each needs its own ads, schedule and budget. Mixing them lets the cheapest searches use up the money the emergencies need.</p>

      <h2>Tight service areas protect expensive clicks</h2>
      <p>At restoration click prices, a call from outside your area is an expensive mistake. Target the area your crews can actually reach quickly, use the location setting that shows ads only to people in that area, and add the names of towns you do not cover as negative keywords. <a href="/learn/google-ads-location-targeting-settings/">Google Ads location targeting</a> explains why the default setting is a common leak.</p>

      <h2>Insurance is part of the sale</h2>
      <p>Many restoration jobs involve an insurance claim, and callers often want to know whether you work with their insurer and can help with the paperwork. If you do, say so in the ads and on the page. It answers the most common question after "how fast can you get here?"</p>

      <h2>Measure jobs won, not calls</h2>
      <p>A call is not a job. Some callers are comparing prices, some are outside the area, some are other contractors. Feeding back which calls became jobs, and their value, from your job management system is what turns this from an expensive channel into a profitable one. Then bidding can favour the searches that produce real work. The <a href="/tools/metrics-calculator/">metrics calculator</a> helps set the cost per job you can afford.</p>

      <h2>Where restoration budgets leak</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Job and certification searches from people looking to work in the industry</li>
        <li>DIY searches: how to dry a carpet, renting a dehumidifier</li>
        <li>Searches about plumbing repairs rather than water damage, unless you do both</li>
        <li>Calls arriving when nobody can answer</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Local Services Ads</h2>
      <p>Where Local Services Ads are available for your category and area, they appear above the normal results and charge per lead. For emergency work they can be very effective alongside Search. Measure them separately and dispute leads that were not genuine enquiries.</p>

      <h2>Where to start</h2>
      <p>If you already advertise, the first check is how many paid calls go unanswered and how many become jobs. A <a href="/google-ads-audit/">$100 account analysis</a> answers both. <a href="#enquiry">Ask for a free audit</a> and tell me your service area and whether you cover calls around the clock.</p>
